</code></pre> <p>Withdrawal of a purchase offer (ICA s.5). In a consumer session every autonomous act is a purchase offer, and s.5 lets the buyer revoke a proposal "at any time before the communication of its acceptance is complete as against the proposer". The buyer therefore MAY send a <code>withdraw</code> that carries <code>accept_msg_hash</code>, the <code>msg_hash</code> of its own <code>accept</code> with <code>legal_effect: purchase_offer</code>, until the seller's <code>on_init</code> receipt. The session moves <code>PURCHASE_OFFERED → NEGOTIATING</code>.</p> <ul> <li>It is allowed only when the named <code>accept</code> has <code>legal_effect: purchase_offer</code>. A <code>withdraw</code> that names an <code>accept</code> with <code>legal_effect: acceptance</code> is refused with <code>accept_revoke_not_allowed</code>.</li> <li>The agent MAY send it without a presence proof, because it is the buyer-favourable direction. The principal MAY also trigger it by the Trusted Surface event <code>negotiation.purchase_offer.withdrawn</code>.</li> <li>It is free: it is a revocation and not a cancellation, so no stage charge attaches. The seller's application shows "buyer withdrew before you confirmed".</li> <li>A crossing with the seller's <code>on_init</code> is decided by the committed-order rule.</li> <li>On Beckn the carrier (an <code>init</code> withdrawn before <code>on_init</code>) is not yet defined (Q-22). Until it is, the principal's only exit on that leg is the decline below.</li> </ul>
